(function(){
const A=window.NLDG_APPLY_CURRENT_EVENTS_V2;if(!A)return;

A({week:11,title:'Economic Inequality',summary:'Scripture does not treat wealth itself as proof of righteousness or poverty as proof of failure. God repeatedly asks how resources, power, work, generosity, and economic systems affect neighbors, especially the vulnerable.',scripture:['Acts 4:32–35','Deuteronomy 15:7–11','James 2:1–9','1 Timothy 6:17–19'],memoryVerse:'Acts 4:34–35',bigIdea:'Christian stewardship asks more than “What am I allowed to keep?” It asks how God’s gifts can be used faithfully, how the poor are treated, whether work and systems are just, and whether money has become a master.',objectives:['Read Acts 4 as Spirit-shaped generosity rather than a slogan.','Identify biblical warnings about both greed and partiality.','Distinguish poverty, wealth, generosity, justice, and economic policy.','Examine personal stewardship and structural concerns without envy or contempt.','Choose one action of generosity, fairness, or advocacy.'],openingQuestion:'When Christians discuss poverty and wealth, why do we so quickly assume the Bible must support our preferred economic system?',sections:[{heading:'The Early Church Saw Needs as Shared Responsibility',type:'scripture',content:'Acts 4 describes believers voluntarily treating possessions as available for the good of the community. The point is not that private property disappears; the point is that love loosens the grip of possession when brothers and sisters are in need.'},{heading:'God Warns Against Hardening the Heart',type:'teaching',content:'Deuteronomy 15 recognizes that poverty persists and commands openhanded generosity rather than using economic arguments as excuses to ignore suffering. Biblical compassion is practical and material.'},{heading:'Partiality Is a Church Problem',type:'discernment',content:'James 2 condemns giving honored treatment to the wealthy while humiliating the poor. Economic status must not become a spiritual ranking system inside the church.'},{heading:'Wealth Is a Stewardship, Not a Verdict',type:'clarity',content:'Scripture includes wealthy faithful people and poor faithful people. Money itself is not the only issue. The recurring questions are how wealth is gained, what it controls, how workers and neighbors are treated, whether generosity is practiced, and where trust is placed.'},{heading:'Jesus Reorders Our Relationship with Possessions',type:'christ',content:'Jesus speaks often about money because it competes for trust and reveals what we value. Following Him may involve generosity, simplicity, restitution, changed business practices, contentment, or costly care for people in need.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Review one week of spending and one place where your work or purchasing choices affect someone with less power. Choose one action: give, share, pay fairly, reduce waste, support a local need, or advocate for a practice that treats people more justly.'}],questions:['What is actually happening in Acts 4, and what is not stated?','Why does James connect favoritism toward wealth with sin?','Can someone be generous personally while participating in unfair economic practices?','What is the difference between concern for inequality and envy?','How can Christians disagree about economic policy without ignoring biblical commands?','What does your use of money currently reveal about trust and priorities?'],prayer:'Provider God, everything we have ultimately belongs to You. Free us from greed, envy, fear, and indifference. Teach us contentment, generosity, fair dealing, and concern for people burdened by poverty. Give us wisdom about both personal choices and the systems we help shape. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Acts and voluntary generosity',content:'Acts describes believers selling property as needs arise and laying proceeds at the apostles’ feet. The narrative emphasizes unity and generosity rather than giving a detailed economic constitution.'},{heading:'Debt and poverty in Deuteronomy',content:'Israel’s law includes debt release, gleaning, wages, and protections designed to keep economic vulnerability from becoming permanent exploitation.'},{heading:'James and status',content:'James confronts churches tempted to honor wealth because it brings social advantage. The gospel overturns status-based favoritism.'}],theology:['Stewardship: possessions are entrusted by God rather than absolute private kingdoms.','Generosity: grace produces openhanded care.','Justice: workers and vulnerable people must not be exploited.','Contentment: abundance and scarcity do not determine worth.','Community: Christian belonging creates responsibilities toward one another.'],worldview:{foundation:'Economic life is morally accountable to God because money, labor, property, debt, wages, and access affect neighbors.',affirm:'Markets, businesses, entrepreneurship, public programs, charity, cooperatives, family networks, and mutual aid can all contribute to human flourishing when ordered justly.',caution:'Any economic system can be distorted by greed, corruption, favoritism, coercion, waste, or indifference to those with less power.',response:'Practice generosity and fair dealing, examine systems honestly, protect the vulnerable, reward meaningful work, and refuse to make money an idol.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Does Acts 4 teach communism?',response:'Acts 4 describes voluntary Spirit-led sharing in a particular church community. It challenges possessiveness powerfully but does not provide a complete modern state economic model.'},{question:'Is poverty always caused by bad choices?',response:'Personal decisions can matter, but Scripture also recognizes famine, disability, oppression, debt, injustice, widowhood, disaster, and exploitation. Mature care avoids both denial of responsibility and simplistic blame.'},{question:'Is inequality itself always unjust?',response:'Different outcomes can arise for many reasons. Scripture focuses strongly on exploitation, favoritism, deprivation, dishonest gain, withheld wages, and neglect of the poor. Christians should evaluate concrete causes and conditions rather than rely only on one statistic.'}],misunderstandings:['Wealth proves God’s favor.','Poverty proves irresponsibility.','Personal charity removes every need to examine systems.','Concern for economic injustice requires resentment toward wealthy people.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Build regular generosity into your budget.','Pay debts and wages honestly where you have control.'],family:['Teach children the difference between wants, needs, gratitude, and generosity.','Include giving decisions in family planning.'],church:['Avoid honoring donors more than people with fewer resources.','Build benevolence with clear, dignifying processes.'],community:['Support fair labor and trustworthy local assistance.','Listen to people experiencing poverty before designing help for them.']}});

A({week:12,title:'Work, Rest, and Burnout',summary:'Work is part of God’s good calling, but humans are not machines. Sabbath, limits, sleep, relationships, and worship remind us that productivity is not our identity.',scripture:['Genesis 2:1–3, 15','Exodus 20:8–11','Mark 6:30–32','Matthew 11:28–30'],memoryVerse:'Matthew 11:28',bigIdea:'Christian faith honors meaningful work and faithful responsibility while rejecting the lie that our worth depends on constant output. Rest is not laziness when it restores us to love God and neighbor well.',objectives:['See both work and rest as part of God’s design.','Distinguish healthy diligence from identity built on productivity.','Recognize common signs and causes of burnout.','Practice limits without using rest as avoidance of responsibility.','Create one sustainable rhythm for work and renewal.'],openingQuestion:'When you stop producing, accomplishing, fixing, or serving, do you still know who you are?',sections:[{heading:'Work Begins Before the Fall',type:'scripture',content:'Genesis presents human work as part of the good creation. Work can express creativity, service, stewardship, provision, and vocation. The curse does not invent work; it makes work painful, frustrated, and vulnerable to exploitation.'},{heading:'God Builds Rest into the Story',type:'teaching',content:'Genesis and the Sabbath command place limits around productivity. Rest declares that the world does not depend entirely on us. We are creatures who need sleep, worship, joy, relationship, and renewal.'},{heading:'Jesus Makes Room for Withdrawal',type:'christ',content:'In Mark 6 Jesus tells tired disciples to come away and rest, even amid real ministry need. Jesus does not treat exhaustion as proof of devotion. At other times He serves sacrificially. Wisdom learns the rhythm between availability and limits.'},{heading:'Burnout Is More Than Being Tired',type:'clarity',content:'Burnout can involve chronic exhaustion, cynicism, reduced effectiveness, emotional depletion, or a sense that everything depends on you. It may arise from workload, caregiving, unhealthy systems, perfectionism, crisis, or lack of support—not simply poor spiritual discipline.'},{heading:'Rest Should Restore Love and Responsibility',type:'discernment',content:'Biblical rest is not avoidance, endless entertainment, or neglect of duties. Healthy rest returns us to God, people, body, and calling with greater freedom rather than deeper numbness.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Choose one protected block of time for worshipful rest. Turn off one source of unnecessary demand, do something restorative, and resist measuring the time by productivity. Also name one workload problem that needs an honest conversation or practical change.'}],questions:['What does Genesis teach about the goodness and limits of work?','Why can rest feel guilty in a productivity-centered culture?','What is the difference between sacrifice and unsustainable overfunctioning?','How can workplaces or churches create burnout, not just individuals?','What kinds of rest actually restore you?','What boundary or support do you need to ask for?'],prayer:'God of work and Sabbath, thank You that our worth is not measured by output. Give us diligence without slavery to achievement, rest without avoidance, and wisdom to recognize our limits. Help employers, churches, and families build rhythms that honor human dignity. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Sabbath as creation and covenant',content:'The Sabbath is rooted both in God’s creation pattern and Israel’s deliverance from slavery. Rest therefore concerns worship, creaturely limits, and freedom from endless production.'},{heading:'Work after Genesis 3',content:'The ground becomes resistant and labor becomes painful. Scripture treats frustration in work as part of a fallen world while still affirming vocation.'},{heading:'Jesus’ invitation',content:'Matthew 11 contrasts burdensome religious weight with Jesus’ yoke. Rest in Christ is discipleship under a gentle Lord, not escape from all responsibility.'}],theology:['Vocation: ordinary work can become service to God and neighbor.','Creatureliness: humans have limits and dependence.','Sabbath: rest resists slavery to production and control.','Grace: worth is received before achievement.','Justice: employers and institutions bear responsibility for humane work conditions.'],worldview:{foundation:'Work matters, but work is not God. Human beings need meaningful labor and meaningful rest.',affirm:'Planning, productivity tools, ambition, career development, entrepreneurship, and high effort can be good when they serve faithful purposes.',caution:'Cultures can glorify overwork, treat people as units of output, or place the cost of unhealthy systems entirely on individuals.',response:'Work diligently, honor limits, protect rest, share burdens, and evaluate whether systems allow people to flourish.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Is burnout just poor boundaries?',response:'Sometimes boundaries contribute, but burnout can also arise from understaffing, caregiving, trauma, low control, injustice, financial necessity, or organizational dysfunction. Solutions may require both personal and systemic change.'},{question:'What if I cannot afford to rest?',response:'Economic reality can severely constrain options. The church should avoid shaming people and instead consider practical support, fair scheduling, childcare, advocacy, shared burdens, and small rhythms of renewal that are actually possible.'},{question:'Can ministry become an idol?',response:'Yes. Serving God can become fused with identity, control, approval, or inability to say no. No ministry role makes a person indispensable to God’s kingdom.'}],misunderstandings:['Rest is laziness.','More exhaustion means more faithfulness.','Burnout is always an individual spiritual failure.','Vacations alone can repair chronic unhealthy systems.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Protect sleep and one recurring rest rhythm where possible.','Notice when identity rises and falls with productivity.'],family:['Share invisible labor more fairly.','Create device-light spaces for rest and relationship.'],church:['Do not build ministry on a few chronically exhausted volunteers.','Normalize sabbath, vacations, delegation, and saying no.'],workplace:['Advocate for realistic workload, breaks, clear expectations, and humane scheduling.','Do not reward constant availability as the only sign of commitment.']}});

A({week:13,title:'Mental Health and the Church',summary:'Scripture makes room for lament, fear, despair, exhaustion, and emotional pain. The church can offer prayer and community while also respecting professional, medical, and crisis care.',scripture:['Psalm 34:17–18','1 Kings 19:1–18','Galatians 6:2','Romans 12:15'],memoryVerse:'Psalm 34:18',bigIdea:'Mental and emotional suffering should not be treated as proof of weak faith. Christians can pray, listen, carry burdens, reduce shame, and connect people with appropriate care while remaining rooted in hope.',objectives:['Recognize biblical examples of deep emotional distress.','Reject simplistic links between mental illness and spiritual failure.','Practice listening without immediately fixing or preaching.','Know when pastoral support should include professional referral.','Create a safer culture for honest struggle.'],openingQuestion:'What makes it easier to tell a church that you have a physical illness than to tell the same church that you are struggling emotionally?',sections:[{heading:'Scripture Does Not Hide Distress',type:'scripture',content:'The Psalms include fear, grief, confusion, anger, loneliness, and despair. The Bible does not preserve only polished spiritual emotions. Lament gives suffering language before God.'},{heading:'Elijah Needed More Than a Lecture',type:'teaching',content:'In 1 Kings 19 Elijah is exhausted and afraid. Before the dramatic spiritual encounter, he sleeps, eats, and receives care. The passage does not reduce his condition to one cause or offer one universal treatment, but it reminds us that body, circumstance, and spirit are connected.'},{heading:'The Church Carries Burdens',type:'application',content:'Galatians 6 calls believers to carry one another’s burdens. That can include listening, meals, transportation, childcare, checking in, helping someone make an appointment, or simply staying present without forcing a quick explanation.'},{heading:'Prayer and Professional Care Are Not Enemies',type:'clarity',content:'Counselors, physicians, therapists, medications, support groups, crisis services, and pastoral care can serve different needs. Seeking qualified help is not automatically evidence of weak faith any more than receiving treatment for another health concern.'},{heading:'Jesus Meets People with Compassion and Truth',type:'christ',content:'Jesus never treats suffering people as inconveniences. He listens, asks questions, touches the excluded, restores dignity, and refuses easy social judgments about why someone suffers.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Practice one conversation in which you listen without trying to solve everything. If someone expresses immediate danger to themselves or another person, do not handle it alone; involve appropriate emergency or crisis support and follow safeguarding procedures.'}],questions:['What emotions are allowed in the Psalms that churches sometimes discourage?','What can we learn from the care Elijah receives?','Why do people sometimes spiritualize mental-health struggles too quickly?','What does good burden-bearing look like in practice?','When should a pastor or friend refer rather than try to be the only helper?','How can your church make honesty safer?'],prayer:'God near to the brokenhearted, teach us to listen well and carry burdens faithfully. Remove shame from people who are struggling. Give wisdom to pastors, families, clinicians, and friends. Help us offer prayer, practical care, truth, patience, and hope without pretending every wound has a quick answer. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Lament in Israel’s worship',content:'Many psalms were prayers for communal worship, showing that sorrow and protest were not excluded from faithful relationship with God.'},{heading:'Elijah’s exhaustion',content:'The narrative follows intense conflict and threat. God’s response includes physical care, presence, correction, renewed calling, and community.'},{heading:'Burden-bearing',content:'Galatians 6 places mutual care inside a community of restoration, humility, and responsibility rather than shame.'}],theology:['Lament: faith can speak honestly from pain.','Embodiment: physical, emotional, relational, and spiritual life interact.','Community: suffering should not be carried alone.','Hope: Christian hope does not require denying present pain.','Wisdom: different kinds of suffering may require different kinds of help.'],worldview:{foundation:'Mental and emotional suffering affects people with strong faith as well as weak faith; dignity remains intact.',affirm:'Evidence-based counseling, medicine, peer support, pastoral care, prayer, healthy routines, and community support can work together appropriately.',caution:'Churches can cause harm by diagnosing people casually, promising cure, discouraging treatment, or treating every symptom as sin or demonic activity.',response:'Listen, reduce shame, pray, support practical needs, respect qualified care, and respond urgently when safety is at risk.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Is depression a sin?',response:'Depression is not a simple moral category. People can experience depressive illness, grief, trauma, exhaustion, or despair for many reasons. Specific behaviors within suffering may still need moral care, but the condition itself should not be reduced to guilt.'},{question:'Can medication be part of Christian care?',response:'Yes. Medication decisions belong with qualified healthcare professionals and the person receiving care. Christians should avoid both stigmatizing medication and treating it as the only possible support.'},{question:'What if someone says they want to die?',response:'Take the statement seriously. Stay with the person if immediate danger is possible, involve emergency or crisis services and trusted responsible adults as appropriate, and follow local safeguarding requirements. Do not promise secrecy.'}],misunderstandings:['Strong Christians do not experience mental illness.','Prayer and therapy compete with each other.','Every emotional struggle has one spiritual cause.','Listening means agreeing with every conclusion a hurting person reaches.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Learn to ask “Do you want me to listen, pray, help solve, or help you find support?”','Avoid casual diagnoses of yourself or others.'],family:['Use non-shaming language about counseling and medication.','Know local crisis and healthcare resources.'],church:['Create referral relationships with qualified professionals.','Train leaders in safeguarding, suicide awareness, and appropriate limits.'],community:['Support accessible mental-health care and crisis resources.','Show up practically for families carrying long-term mental-health burdens.']}});

A({week:14,title:'Violence in the News',summary:'Repeated exposure to violence can produce fear, rage, numbness, and tribal thinking. Jesus calls His people to grieve honestly, protect the vulnerable, refuse vengeance, and become peacemakers.',scripture:['Romans 12:17–21','Matthew 5:9, 38–48','Psalm 11','James 1:19–20'],memoryVerse:'Romans 12:21',bigIdea:'Christian response to violence is neither passive indifference nor revenge. We tell the truth about evil, protect people where we can, pursue justice, pray for enemies, and refuse to let hatred reproduce itself in us.',objectives:['Distinguish grief, anger, justice, protection, and revenge.','Practice responding to violent news without feeding panic or dehumanization.','Understand Jesus’ call to enemy-love as costly discipleship.','Recognize legitimate Christian disagreement about force and public protection.','Choose one act of peacemaking or support.'],openingQuestion:'What happens inside us when we consume story after story of violence without time to grieve, pray, or process?',sections:[{heading:'Do Not Repay Evil for Evil',type:'scripture',content:'Romans 12 does not deny that evil is real. It refuses personal vengeance and calls believers to overcome evil with good. The Christian response must not become a copy of the hatred it opposes.'},{heading:'Anger Can Signal Wrong but Cannot Rule Us',type:'discernment',content:'James warns that human anger does not automatically produce God’s righteousness. Anger may alert us to injustice, but it needs truth, self-control, wise process, and love before it becomes action.'},{heading:'Jesus Calls Peacemakers Blessed',type:'christ',content:'Peacemaking is not pretending conflict does not exist. Jesus confronts evil while rejecting hatred as the identity of His followers. Enemy-love refuses to surrender another person’s humanity even when strong boundaries or consequences are necessary.'},{heading:'Protection and Vengeance Are Different Questions',type:'clarity',content:'Christians have long disagreed about pacifism, just-war reasoning, policing, self-defense, and the use of force. The traditions share important concerns: force must never be loved, vengeance is forbidden, innocent life matters, and power requires moral accountability.'},{heading:'News Consumption Forms the Heart',type:'application',content:'Graphic repetition, sensational framing, and constant alerts can intensify fear or numb compassion. Staying informed can be responsible; compulsive exposure is not required for faithfulness.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Choose one violent event you are carrying. Pray for victims, families, responders, offenders, and leaders. Verify information before sharing it. Then support one concrete peace-building, victim-support, safety, or restorative effort.'}],questions:['What is the difference between justice and vengeance in Romans 12?','How can anger help and harm our response to violence?','What does enemy-love require without eliminating boundaries?','Why have faithful Christians disagreed about the use of force?','How does violent media affect your emotions and relationships?','What is one form of peacemaking available to you?'],prayer:'Prince of Peace, we grieve the violence people suffer and inflict. Comfort victims, restrain evil, guide those responsible for public safety, and keep hatred from taking root in us. Make us truthful, courageous, protective, merciful, and committed to overcoming evil with good. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Romans 12 and vengeance',content:'Paul addresses a community called to bless persecutors, live peaceably where possible, and leave ultimate vengeance to God.'},{heading:'Sermon on the Mount',content:'Jesus intensifies neighbor-love by including enemies and forbidding retaliation as a controlling ethic for disciples.'},{heading:'Christian traditions on force',content:'Historic churches have included pacifist traditions and just-war traditions. Both wrestle with Jesus’ teaching, public responsibility, protection of the innocent, and limits on violence.'}],theology:['Peace: God’s kingdom aims toward restored relationships and the end of violence.','Justice: evil should be named and restrained.','Enemy-love: opponents remain image-bearers.','Government: public authorities bear responsibilities distinct from personal vengeance.','Hope: Christians refuse despair because violence does not have the final word.'],worldview:{foundation:'Violence is a tragic expression of human sin and brokenness, not entertainment or a tool to celebrate casually.',affirm:'Safety planning, lawful protection, trauma care, violence prevention, restorative practices, and responsible public institutions can all serve peace.',caution:'Fear and outrage can be manipulated to justify dehumanization, collective blame, or excessive force.',response:'Grieve, verify facts, protect the vulnerable, seek just accountability, refuse vengeance, and practice peacemaking.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Does loving enemies mean refusing all self-defense?',response:'Christians answer differently. Any position should take Jesus’ command seriously, reject revenge, consider protection of others, use the least harmful means reasonably available, and remain accountable for how force is used.'},{question:'Can Christians support police or military service?',response:'Many Christian traditions say yes under moral constraints; others practice conscientious nonviolence. The church should teach the ethical seriousness of such roles rather than romanticizing violence.'},{question:'Is anger at injustice wrong?',response:'Not automatically. Scripture includes righteous anger, but anger becomes dangerous when it abandons truth, proportion, self-control, dignity, or love.'}],misunderstandings:['Peacemaking means passivity.','Justice requires hatred of the offender.','Being informed requires watching graphic coverage repeatedly.','All Christians have held one view of war and force.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Set limits on repeated violent media exposure.','Refuse to share unverified graphic or inflammatory content.'],family:['Discuss frightening events honestly at an age-appropriate level.','Teach children safety without teaching contempt for groups of people.'],church:['Provide trauma-aware prayer and support after community violence.','Avoid glorifying weapons, war, or revenge in Christian language.'],community:['Support victim services and violence-prevention efforts.','Build relationships that reduce isolation and retaliatory cycles.']}});

A({week:15,title:'Social Media and the Tongue',summary:'Digital words travel farther and remain longer, but Jesus’ call is unchanged: speak truthfully, resist contempt and gossip, and use words to give grace rather than destroy.',scripture:['James 3:1–12','Ephesians 4:25–32','Proverbs 18:21','Matthew 12:33–37'],memoryVerse:'Ephesians 4:29',bigIdea:'The screen does not suspend discipleship. Posts, comments, shares, jokes, private messages, and reactions are part of the speech for which Christians are responsible.',objectives:['Connect James’ teaching about the tongue with digital communication.','Identify gossip, exaggeration, contempt, and impulsive posting.','Practice truth, timing, tone, and restraint.','Learn when silence, private conversation, or correction is wiser than public response.','Choose one digital speech habit to change.'],openingQuestion:'Would you say your last ten online comments the same way if the person were sitting across the table?',sections:[{heading:'James Describes Small Words with Large Consequences',type:'scripture',content:'James compares the tongue to a bit, rudder, and fire. Digital tools amplify the same reality: a few seconds of speech can shape reputations, relationships, workplaces, churches, and whole communities.'},{heading:'Truth Matters Before Tone',type:'discernment',content:'A gracious tone cannot make a false claim true. Before sharing, ask whether the claim is accurate, whether context is missing, and whether you are repeating someone else’s accusation without evidence.'},{heading:'Tone Matters Even When the Claim Is True',type:'application',content:'Ephesians 4 asks whether speech builds up and gives grace. Sarcasm, mockery, humiliation, dogpiling, and contempt can turn a true concern into destructive communication.'},{heading:'Not Every Thought Needs an Audience',type:'clarity',content:'Wisdom includes timing and silence. Public correction is sometimes necessary, but many conflicts are better handled privately, after emotions cool, or not at all. Deleting a draft can be an act of discipleship.'},{heading:'Jesus Connects Speech with the Heart',type:'christ',content:'Jesus teaches that words reveal what fills the heart. Online behavior therefore becomes a spiritual mirror. The issue is not only platform etiquette but what anger, pride, fear, insecurity, or love is being formed inside us.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Use a 60-second pause before posting anything emotionally charged. Ask: Is it true? Is it necessary? Is it mine to share? Is this the right setting? Does the tone honor Christ and the person involved?'}],questions:['What makes digital speech feel less accountable than face-to-face speech?','Can a true statement still be sinful speech?','What is the difference between warning people and gossiping?','When is public correction appropriate?','What do your online reactions reveal about your heart?','Which digital speech habit needs repentance or restraint?'],prayer:'Jesus, Lord of our words, forgive careless, false, proud, and cruel speech. Give us courage to tell truth, wisdom to know when and where to speak, humility to listen, and self-control to remain silent when our words would only wound. Let our digital lives belong to You. Amen.',background:[{heading:'James and community conflict',content:'James addresses teachers, quarrels, partiality, and uncontrolled desires. Speech is treated as a central measure of maturity in community.'},{heading:'Ephesians and the new humanity',content:'Ephesians 4 places truthful, gracious speech inside the practical life of a reconciled people.'},{heading:'Public permanence',content:'Modern platforms can preserve, copy, and redistribute words far beyond the original audience, increasing the responsibility attached to impulsive speech.'}],theology:['Speech: words can bless, harm, reveal, and create social reality.','Truth: Christians must not knowingly spread falsehood.','Sanctification: self-control includes digital habits.','Neighbor-love: the person on the other side of the screen remains an image-bearer.','Repentance: correction includes deleting, apologizing, and repairing misinformation.'],worldview:{foundation:'Digital communication is real human communication and remains morally accountable to Christ.',affirm:'Social media can strengthen relationships, spread helpful information, organize service, teach, encourage, and give overlooked people a voice.',caution:'Platforms can reward outrage, speed, humiliation, comparison, and certainty more than truth and wisdom.',response:'Slow down, verify, choose the right setting, speak with dignity, and repair harm when your words fail.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Is calling out public wrongdoing gossip?',response:'Not necessarily. Public accountability can be appropriate when claims are verified, the issue is genuinely public, vulnerable people need protection, and the goal is truth and justice rather than entertainment or humiliation.'},{question:'Should Christians debate online?',response:'Sometimes. Ask whether the medium supports understanding, whether the audience needs the exchange, whether you can remain self-controlled, and whether the conversation is producing any good.'},{question:'What if someone posts something offensive about me?',response:'You may respond, document, block, report, seek help, or address it privately depending on safety and context. Forgiveness does not require unlimited access or public silence.'}],misunderstandings:['If a statement is true, tone does not matter.','Private messages are morally less serious than public posts.','Silence always means cowardice.','Deleting a harmful post is enough without correcting misinformation or apologizing.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Audit your last week of posts and comments for fruit.','Unfollow accounts that primarily train you in contempt.'],family:['Set norms for respectful group chats and posting others’ images or stories.','Teach children not to forward rumors or humiliating content.'],church:['Create a clear social-media conduct standard for leaders.','Handle complaints without online retaliation.'],community:['Use digital platforms to highlight service, truth, and constructive local information.','Do not amplify public shaming for entertainment.']}});

A({week:16,title:'Addiction to Technology',summary:'Technology can serve us, but anything that repeatedly controls attention, time, sleep, relationships, or obedience deserves serious examination. Christian freedom includes the ability to say no.',scripture:['1 Corinthians 6:12','Ephesians 5:15–17','Mark 1:35','Matthew 6:19–24'],memoryVerse:'1 Corinthians 6:12',bigIdea:'Not every heavy technology habit is a clinical addiction, but Paul’s question is still searching: “Will I be mastered by anything?” Discipleship includes stewardship of attention, desire, time, and embodied relationships.',objectives:['Use careful language about habit, compulsion, and addiction.','Identify ways technology can master attention or routine.','Connect digital habits with sleep, relationships, prayer, and responsibility.','Practice boundaries that create freedom rather than shame.','Choose one measurable change for the week.'],openingQuestion:'What device or app is hardest for you to put down when you already know you should stop?',sections:[{heading:'Christian Freedom Includes Not Being Mastered',type:'scripture',content:'In 1 Corinthians 6 Paul refuses a shallow idea of freedom. Something can be technically permissible while still gaining unhealthy control. The question is not only “Is this allowed?” but “What is this doing to me?”'},{heading:'Attention Is a Form of Stewardship',type:'teaching',content:'Every hour of attention is an hour unavailable somewhere else. Technology competes with sleep, prayer, reading, work, family, exercise, silence, and face-to-face presence. The issue is not that screens are automatically bad but that attention is limited.'},{heading:'Use Accurate Language About Addiction',type:'clarity',content:'People often call any strong habit an addiction. Clinical behavioral disorders require careful assessment. A Bible study should not diagnose people casually. We can still name loss of control, withdrawal from responsibilities, compulsive use, or harmful patterns that deserve support.'},{heading:'Jesus Practiced Deliberate Withdrawal',type:'christ',content:'The Gospels show Jesus leaving crowds and demands to pray. If the Son of God did not treat every incoming need as an immediate command, disciples can also create boundaries around constant availability.'},{heading:'Boundaries Reveal What We Believe',type:'application',content:'Turning off notifications, charging devices outside the bedroom, using app limits, keeping meals screen-free, or taking a regular digital sabbath are not magic rules. They are ways of training freedom and making room for what matters more.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Choose one behavior you can measure for seven days: no phone during meals, no social media for the first 30 minutes after waking, devices outside the bedroom, or one evening offline. Notice what emotions appear when the habit is interrupted.'}],questions:['What does Paul mean by refusing to be mastered?','Which digital habits are useful, and which feel compulsory?','What parts of life lose attention when screens gain it?','Why should we avoid diagnosing every heavy user as addicted?','What did Jesus model about availability and withdrawal?','Which boundary would create more freedom for you?'],prayer:'Lord, our time and attention belong to You. Show us where useful tools have become controlling habits. Give us self-control without shame, wisdom without fear of technology, and courage to create space for prayer, sleep, work, family, friendship, and service. Amen.',background:[{heading:'“I will not be mastered”',content:'Paul addresses embodied holiness and misuse of freedom in Corinth. The principle reaches beyond the original issue: Christian freedom is incompatible with slavery to appetite.'},{heading:'Attention economy',content:'Many digital services are designed to increase time and return visits. Christians do not need to demonize design in order to recognize that incentives can work against intentional use.'},{heading:'Jesus and solitude',content:'Jesus alternates intense ministry with prayerful withdrawal. Solitude is not escapism when it restores communion and calling.'}],theology:['Freedom: grace liberates people for obedience, not endless appetite.','Stewardship: time and attention are limited gifts.','Embodiment: sleep, movement, presence, and physical community matter.','Self-control: Spirit-formed maturity includes limits.','Idolatry: good tools become rivals when they claim trust, identity, or mastery.'],worldview:{foundation:'Technology should remain a servant of human flourishing and faithful responsibility rather than master of attention.',affirm:'Devices can support work, accessibility, education, relationship, ministry, navigation, creativity, and safety.',caution:'Design incentives, social pressure, boredom avoidance, loneliness, and habit loops can make intentional use difficult.',response:'Measure patterns honestly, build practical boundaries, seek help when control is seriously impaired, and replace compulsive use with richer practices rather than mere deprivation.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'How do I know whether I am addicted?',response:'Loss of control, persistent harm, failed attempts to cut back, interference with responsibilities, and significant distress can be warning signs, but diagnosis belongs with qualified professionals when a clinical disorder is suspected.'},{question:'Are smartphones spiritually harmful?',response:'A device is not automatically holy or sinful. Patterns of use can support or undermine truth, presence, rest, attention, sexuality, generosity, and community.'},{question:'What if my job requires constant connection?',response:'Some roles have legitimate demands, but clear expectations, rotating coverage, emergency definitions, and protected off-hours can still matter. Organizational culture may need change, not only personal discipline.'}],misunderstandings:['Every frequent technology user is addicted.','The only faithful solution is deleting every app.','Self-control means relying on willpower without changing environment.','Technology problems belong only to teenagers.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Turn off nonessential notifications.','Track screen use without self-condemnation and look for patterns.'],family:['Create shared device boundaries adults follow too.','Keep bedrooms and meals from becoming default screen zones where possible.'],church:['Do not require volunteers to be reachable at all hours.','Teach digital habits without panic or generational stereotypes.'],workplace:['Clarify after-hours expectations.','Avoid unnecessary notifications and meetings that train constant interruption.']}});

A({week:17,title:'Online Influence and Identity',summary:'Online platforms invite us to perform a version of ourselves for approval. Jesus calls disciples to live before the Father rather than building identity from likes, reach, image, or public recognition.',scripture:['Matthew 6:1–6','Galatians 1:10','John 12:42–43','1 Thessalonians 2:3–8'],memoryVerse:'Galatians 1:10',bigIdea:'Influence is not automatically wrong, but approval becomes spiritually dangerous when we begin shaping truth, generosity, appearance, outrage, or even faith around what receives attention.',objectives:['Recognize the difference between influence and identity built on approval.','Read Matthew 6 as a warning about performing righteousness for an audience.','Identify pressures to curate a false or incomplete self.','Practice integrity between public and private life.','Choose one act of faithfulness that does not need an audience.'],openingQuestion:'If nobody could like, share, comment on, or publicly see something good you did, would you still want to do it?',sections:[{heading:'Jesus Warns About Audience-Driven Righteousness',type:'scripture',content:'Matthew 6 does not condemn giving, prayer, or public life. Jesus confronts motives shaped by being seen and praised. A good act can become spiritually distorted when the audience becomes the real reward.'},{heading:'Influence Is a Stewardship, Not an Identity',type:'teaching',content:'Teachers, artists, leaders, creators, pastors, parents, and ordinary users all influence people. Influence becomes unhealthy when numbers determine worth or when the desire to keep an audience begins controlling what we say and who we pretend to be.'},{heading:'Curated Life Is Not Whole Life',type:'discernment',content:'Profiles display selected moments. Comparing your unedited life to someone else’s chosen highlights produces a distorted comparison. Integrity does not require sharing everything; it requires refusing deliberate deception.'},{heading:'Approval Can Quiet Conviction',type:'christ',content:'John 12 describes leaders who believed but feared losing human approval. Jesus calls people to a loyalty deeper than reputation. Sometimes faithfulness costs applause.'},{heading:'The Gospel Frees Us from Constant Performance',type:'application',content:'Grace means we do not have to manufacture worth through visibility. We can do hidden good, admit mistakes, resist outrage trends, and let ordinary faithfulness matter even when it produces no engagement.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Do one meaningful act of generosity, prayer, encouragement, or service without posting about it. Then notice what the absence of an audience reveals about your motives and sense of worth.'}],questions:['What motive does Jesus expose in Matthew 6?','When does healthy influence become dependence on approval?','What is the danger of comparing real life to curated life?','How can online identity tempt people to hide necessary repentance?','What might faithfulness cost in terms of popularity?','What good are you willing to do without recognition?'],prayer:'Father who sees in secret, free us from living for applause. Give us integrity when visible and invisible, courage when truth costs popularity, and gratitude for influence without making it our identity. Teach us to seek Your pleasure above the crowd’s approval. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Honor culture',content:'Jesus taught in a world where public honor and shame carried enormous social weight. Matthew 6 challenges religious practices performed to secure human praise.'},{heading:'Galatians and people-pleasing',content:'Paul contrasts serving Christ with shaping the gospel around human approval.'},{heading:'Public discipleship',content:'The New Testament includes public witness, but visibility is always subordinate to faithfulness rather than treated as proof of success.'}],theology:['Grace: worth is received from God rather than audience response.','Integrity: public and private selves should increasingly align.','Humility: influence is entrusted rather than possessed.','Witness: visibility can serve the gospel when motive and method remain faithful.','Idolatry: approval becomes a rival god when it controls obedience.'],worldview:{foundation:'Human attention is not a reliable measure of truth, worth, or spiritual fruit.',affirm:'Online influence can teach, encourage, mobilize generosity, build community, and amplify voices that might otherwise be unheard.',caution:'Metrics can reward image, outrage, certainty, beauty, wealth, or performance rather than wisdom and character.',response:'Use influence to serve, protect private faithfulness, disclose honestly, resist manufactured personas, and accept that obedience may reduce popularity.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Is it wrong for ministries to track views or engagement?',response:'No. Metrics can help evaluate reach and communication. They become dangerous when they replace deeper measures such as truthfulness, discipleship, care, repentance, community, and long-term fruit.'},{question:'Is posting good works always showing off?',response:'Not necessarily. Public stories can inspire, report accountability, or invite participation. Jesus’ warning centers on motive and reward. Ask whether the post serves people and God or mainly builds the poster’s image.'},{question:'How authentic should I be online?',response:'Authenticity does not require total disclosure. Privacy and boundaries are healthy. The goal is not sharing everything but refusing to create a knowingly false identity.'}],misunderstandings:['Large influence proves God’s favor.','Private faithfulness does not matter if nobody sees it.','Authenticity means sharing every personal detail.','Any public ministry activity is automatically performative.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Take regular breaks from checking engagement metrics.','Practice hidden acts of faithfulness.'],family:['Avoid tying children’s worth to public achievements or online popularity.','Ask permission before posting personal family stories.'],church:['Celebrate quiet service alongside visible platform roles.','Measure ministry fruit beyond attendance and social reach.'],community:['Use platforms to amplify needs without exploiting vulnerable people’s stories.','Share credit and direct attention toward the people or causes being served.']}});

A({week:18,title:'Misinformation and Discernment',summary:'Christians are responsible not only for what we believe but for what we repeat. Biblical discernment values truth, while factual discernment asks for sources, evidence, context, and correction.',scripture:['Proverbs 14:15','Proverbs 18:13, 17','1 Thessalonians 5:21–22','Acts 17:10–12','Ephesians 4:25'],memoryVerse:'Proverbs 14:15',bigIdea:'Discernment is not automatic suspicion. It is the disciplined refusal to believe or share a claim merely because it is emotional, familiar, flattering, frightening, or useful to our side.',objectives:['Distinguish biblical discernment from factual verification.','Use Proverbs 18 to value hearing evidence before judging.','Recognize confirmation bias and emotionally manipulative claims.','Practice source-checking and correction.','Refuse to use Scripture as a substitute for evidence about factual claims.'],openingQuestion:'Have you ever shared something because it fit what you already believed, only to learn later that it was false or misleading?',sections:[{heading:'The Prudent Person Does Not Believe Everything',type:'scripture',content:'Proverbs 14:15 contrasts gullibility with thoughtful steps. Christian trust is not a command to become easy to deceive. Wisdom slows down before accepting claims.'},{heading:'Hear More Than the First Version',type:'discernment',content:'Proverbs 18 warns against answering before listening and reminds us that the first case can sound convincing until another perspective is heard. This applies to interpersonal conflict, headlines, edited clips, accusations, and viral stories.'},{heading:'Biblical and Factual Questions Are Different',type:'clarity',content:'Scripture tells us to love truth, reject false witness, and test teaching. But the Bible does not tell us whether a particular photo is edited, statistic is accurate, quote is authentic, or event happened as claimed. Those questions require evidence and reliable sources.'},{heading:'Why We Believe What We Want to Believe',type:'worldview',content:'People naturally notice information that confirms existing beliefs and resist information that threatens identity or loyalty. Christians are not immune. Discernment begins by admitting that our own side can mislead us too.'},{heading:'Jesus Is Not Served by Falsehood',type:'christ',content:'A false claim does not become righteous because it supports a Christian cause. Bearing false witness remains wrong even when the target is someone we distrust. Loyalty to Jesus requires correction when our preferred narrative is untrue.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Before sharing a factual claim, identify the original source, check whether credible independent sources confirm it, read beyond the headline, note the date and context, and ask whether you would apply the same standard if the claim criticized your own side.'}],questions:['What does Proverbs 14 praise instead of gullibility?','Why is the first version of a story often incomplete?','What is the difference between asking “Is this biblical?” and “Did this happen?”','Why does confirmation bias affect Christians too?','What responsibility do we have after sharing something false?','What standard of evidence do you use for claims you dislike versus claims you like?'],prayer:'God of truth, make us slow to believe what is convenient and quick to correct what is false. Give us patience to investigate, humility to admit mistakes, courage to resist our own tribe, and love strong enough to refuse false witness. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Wisdom literature',content:'Proverbs forms people who listen, compare, seek counsel, and avoid hasty judgment. Discernment is practical moral wisdom.'},{heading:'The Bereans',content:'Acts 17 praises people who receive Paul’s message eagerly while examining Scripture. Trust and testing are not opposites.'},{heading:'False witness',content:'Biblical ethics treats false testimony as a serious neighbor-love issue because false claims can damage reputation, justice, and community.'}],theology:['Truth: God’s character opposes falsehood.','Wisdom: faithful judgment requires patience and evidence.','Humility: Christians can be mistaken and manipulated.','Neighbor-love: misinformation can harm real people.','Repentance: truthfulness includes public correction when appropriate.'],worldview:{foundation:'Truth does not change according to which group benefits from a claim.',affirm:'Journalism, primary documents, scientific methods, fact-checking, eyewitness testimony, public records, and expert analysis can all contribute evidence when evaluated carefully.',caution:'No source is infallible; distrust can become as irrational as gullibility when people reject every institution and believe only voices inside their tribe.',response:'Use proportional confidence, seek primary evidence, compare reliable sources, distinguish fact from interpretation, and correct errors.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'How do I know which sources to trust?',response:'Look for transparent sourcing, corrections, relevant expertise, direct evidence, separation of news and opinion, and confirmation by independent sources. Trust should be calibrated rather than absolute.'},{question:'What if experts disagree?',response:'Ask whether disagreement is broad or narrow, which claims have stronger evidence, whether experts are speaking within their field, and what level of confidence the evidence supports. Uncertainty is not the same as ignorance.'},{question:'Is “the Bible says” enough to prove a news claim?',response:'No. Scripture gives moral and theological truth, but factual claims about current events require factual evidence. Using a verse to bypass evidence can misuse Scripture.'}],misunderstandings:['Discernment means distrusting everything.','A Christian source is automatically accurate.','If a claim supports a good cause, accuracy matters less.','Deleting a false post quietly always fulfills the duty to correct it.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Wait before reposting emotionally charged claims.','Correct misinformation you have spread when people may still be affected.'],family:['Teach children how to find an original source.','Compare headlines with full articles together.'],church:['Verify illustrations, statistics, quotes, and viral stories before preaching them.','Never use fabricated or unverified stories because they preach well.'],community:['Model fair standards of evidence across political and social divides.','Support local information sources that practice transparent correction.']}});
})();
